# Structured Logging & Observability

## Core Principle

Use structured logging in JSON format with trace IDs, span IDs, and correlation IDs. This enables effective debugging, monitoring, and distributed tracing.

## Structured Log Format

### Required Fields

All logs must include:

```typescript
interface StructuredLog {
  timestamp: string;        // ISO 8601 format
  level: string;            // error, warn, info, debug
  message: string;          // Human-readable message
  traceId: string;          // W3C Trace Context trace ID
  spanId?: string;          // Current span ID
  service: string;          // Service name
  environment: string;      // prod, staging, dev
  
  // Request context
  correlationId?: string;   // Request correlation ID
  userId?: string;          // User ID (if applicable)
  requestId?: string;       // HTTP request ID
  
  // Error context (for errors)
  error?: {
    name: string;
    message: string;
    stack?: string;
  };
  
  // Additional context
  [key: string]: any;       // Custom fields
}
```

## W3C Trace Context

Use W3C Trace Context standard for distributed tracing:

```
traceparent: 00-{trace-id}-{parent-id}-{trace-flags}
```

- **trace-id**: 32 hex characters (16 bytes)
- **parent-id**: 16 hex characters (8 bytes)
- **trace-flags**: 2 hex characters (sampling flag)

### Propagation

- Extract `traceparent` from incoming HTTP headers
- Generate new trace ID if not present
- Pass trace context to downstream services
- Include in all log entries

## RED Metrics (Services)

Track **Rate, Errors, Duration** for all services:

- **Rate**: Requests per second
- **Errors**: Error rate (errors / total requests)
- **Duration**: Request latency (p50, p95, p99)

```typescript
interface REDMetrics {
  rate: number;              // requests/second
  errors: number;            // errors/second
  errorRate: number;         // errors / total (percentage)
  duration: {
    p50: number;             // 50th percentile latency (ms)
    p95: number;             // 95th percentile latency (ms)
    p99: number;             // 99th percentile latency (ms)
    avg: number;             // Average latency (ms)
  };
}
```

## USE Metrics (Resources)

Track **Utilization, Saturation, Errors** for resources:

- **Utilization**: Percentage of time resource is busy
- **Saturation**: Degree of queuing (queue length)
- **Errors**: Error count or rate

## Log Levels

- **ERROR**: System errors requiring attention
- **WARN**: Warning conditions that may need attention
- **INFO**: Informational messages (request logs, state changes)
- **DEBUG**: Detailed debug information (development only)

## DO

- ✅ Use JSON format for all logs
- ✅ Include trace ID in every log entry
- ✅ Include correlation ID for request tracking
- ✅ Log structured error objects (not just strings)
- ✅ Never log PII, secrets, or sensitive data
- ✅ Use appropriate log levels
- ✅ Include relevant context (user ID, request path, etc.)
- ✅ Send logs to centralized system (OpenTelemetry Collector)

## DON'T

- ❌ Use unstructured string concatenation
- ❌ Log sensitive information (passwords, API keys, PII)
- ❌ Log full stack traces in production (log error name/message)
- ❌ Use console.log in production
- ❌ Mix log formats (always use JSON)
- ❌ Include stack traces in info-level logs

## OpenTelemetry Integration

Use OpenTelemetry for unified telemetry:

```typescript
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';

const tracer = trace.getTracer('my-service');

async function processRequest() {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('processRequest');
  
  try {
    // Your code here
    span.setAttribute('user.id', userId);
    span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.OK });
  } catch (error) {
    span.setStatus({ 
      code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR,
      message: error.message 
    });
    span.recordException(error);
    throw error;
  } finally {
    span.end();
  }
}
```

## PII Redaction

Never log PII. Use redaction patterns:

- Email addresses
- Phone numbers
- Social security numbers
- Credit card numbers
- IP addresses (if required by policy)

## Example Log Entry

```json
{
  "timestamp": "2025-11-27T21:30:00.000Z",
